      "Juárez, refusing to recognize Maximilian's legitimacy, led a prolonged resistance government operating from Mexico's northern regions throughout the French occupation. With the American Civil War concluded by 1865, the United States began providing crucial diplomatic pressure and material support to Juárez's cause, while Napoleon III, facing mounting European pressures, withdrew French troops by 1867. Maximilian, abandoned by his French sponsors, was captured by Juárez's forces and, despite international pleas for clemency (including from Victor Hugo), executed by firing squad in June 1867 — a decisive assertion of Mexican sovereignty. Juárez, subsequently reelected president, continued pursuing liberal reforms until his death in office in 1872, remembered today as one of Mexico's most consequential leaders — a symbol of Indigenous achievement, liberal reform, and unwavering national sovereignty against foreign imperial ambition."
